# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-accoreconsole-console-pipe-not-drained — a long accoreconsole run may block on its own console pipe (suspected)
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: windows-access (console-flood:probe:accoreconsole:windows to confirm on the AutoCAD runner)
#+BRANCH: fix-alfe-drain-engine-pipes

* Context

Found while harvesting option keywords (cadtui-locale-option-keywords.issue,
2026-10-08). The optkw probe enters 13 commands in ONE accoreconsole session
(alfe --autocad --mode batch). Three runs hung part-way, each after a
DIFFERENT command:

| job         | order                                   | completed                    | hung at |
|-------------+-----------------------------------------+------------------------------+---------|
| 16981155886 | OFFSET..SCALE, ZOOM, RECTANG..          | 8 (OFFSET..SCALE)            | ZOOM    |
| 16998925798 | OFFSET..SCALE, RECTANG, PLINE.., ZOOM   | 8 (OFFSET..SCALE)            | RECTANG |
| 17026576122 | PLINE BREAK LENGTHEN OFFSET..SCALE ..   | 9 (PLINE..MIRROR)            | ROTATE  |

In each run every completed command's log carries the same second as the
start, and then the job sits silent until alfe's --timeout 300 expires (the
probe step lasts ~5 min 20 s). So it is a hang, not a crash, and it is
neither a particular command (ROTATE completed after MIRROR in the first two
runs; it hung after MIRROR in the third) nor the Nth command (8 vs 9). The
same probe on BricsCAD V25 Windows (job 17026576124) completes all 13.

* Problem (suspected cause -- not yet proven)

backend-autocad.lisp launches accoreconsole with =:output :stream
:error-output :stream= (START-ENGINE, CALL-LAUNCHER) and reads those pipes
only once the process is DEAD (PROCESS-EXIT-DETAILS,
LAUNCHER-FAILURE-DETAILS). Nothing reads them while the engine runs: the
payload comes through the file protocol. accoreconsole writes its whole
command line to that console (UTF-16LE: every command echo, prompt and cancel
-- 2 bytes a character). Once the pipe's buffer is full, accoreconsole's next
console write blocks for ever: the hang depends on the cumulative console
OUTPUT, which is what the three runs show (a fixed amount reached at a
different command depending on what came before). The output volumes before
the three hang points are close (about 3.6k-4.0k characters of probe output)
but my estimate of what the console itself receives is too rough to pin one
capacity, hence "suspected".

Any long accoreconsole run with chatty commands (command-driving probes,
user scripts run through alfe --mode batch) is exposed.

* Proposal

1. Confirm: a probe that only PRINCs (no command) N kilobytes in one
   accoreconsole session, for N = 2, 4, 8, 16: a cliff at a fixed N confirms
   the pipe; no cliff refutes it.
2. Fix: drain accoreconsole's stdout/stderr WHILE it runs -- a reader thread
   per pipe accumulating into a buffer (kept for the diagnostics that now
   read the pipes after exit), or launch with =:output= / =:error-output=
   pointing at files in the workdir and read those after exit.
3. Meanwhile the optkw wrapper runs accoreconsole one command per session
   (scripts/run-optkw-probe.ps1, OPTKW_COMMANDS), which keeps each session's
   output small.

* Acceptance criteria

- The N-kilobyte probe (or the optkw probe in ONE session) completes on
  AutoCAD 2022's accoreconsole.
- The launch diagnostics (exit details, failure details) still show the
  console text.

* Resolution (clautolisp 2.3.11 / alfe 2.3.11, 2026-10-08, branch fix-alfe-drain-engine-pipes) -- fixed, awaiting the runner probe

** The mechanism, demonstrated off the runner

A regression test runs a REAL child process (the host Lisp) through
START-ENGINE's own launch keywords; the child writes 1 MiB to its standard
output and 1 MiB to its standard error before it does its job. Against the
code as it was (pipes read only after exit) all four variants -- AutoCAD and
BricsCAD start paths, READY and exit-before-READY -- BLOCKED in the middle of
the flood and failed on the 30 s READY timeout (146 s for the four, on
poseidon/SBCL); with the fix they pass in about 2 s. So the hang is real for
any engine that writes more than a pipe buffer to its console, which is what
accoreconsole does. Whether THAT is what stopped the three optkw runs is for
the probe below to confirm on AutoCAD 2022.

** Audit -- every place alfe (and clautolisp) starts a child

| site                                                                   | what it did                                         | now |
|------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+-----|
| backend-autocad START-ENGINE (accoreconsole batch, cscript bridge)     | :output/:error-output :stream, read after exit only | FIXED: files in the workdir |
| backend-autocad QUIT-CREATED-AUTOCAD (quit cscript)                    | :output/:error-output :stream, never read           | FIXED: :output nil :error-output nil (discarded) |
| backend-bricscad START-ENGINE (bricscad batch, cscript, osascript)     | :output/:error-output :stream, read after non-zero exit only | FIXED: files in the workdir |
| backend-clautolisp %RUN-ENGINE-CHILD (alfe -> clautolisp-sbcl child)   | output/error to temporary FILES, or :interactive    | already safe (unchanged) |
| backend-autocad %RUN-CAPTURING-TEXT (reg.exe), trustedpaths %RUN-POWERSHELL, cad-common AppleLanguages (defaults read) | uiop:run-program :output :string, :error-output nil | safe: RUN-PROGRAM drains while it waits (unchanged) |
| clautolisp autolisp-dcl subprocess-renderer (Qt GUI driver)            | :input/:output :stream, :error-output :interactive  | safe: a request/response protocol reads its output (unchanged) |
| clautolisp autolisp-file-compat product runner                         | :output *standard-output* :error-output *error-output*, then wait-process | safe: the copy runs during WAIT-PROCESS (unchanged) |
| clautolisp builtins startapp, vle-startapp, doc-clipboard (api.lisp)   | :output nil :error-output nil, or run-program       | safe (unchanged) |
| clautolisp debug-ui-dumb pager, tools/clautolisp shell escape, sedit clipboard, pathname-mapping uname/mount, terminal-color | run-program with :interactive / :string / a string input | safe (unchanged) |

The scripts that drive alfe (scripts/run-*.ps1, run-*.sh) read alfe's own
output through PowerShell / shell pipelines that are consumed as they run;
not affected. Nothing under clautolisp/cador was touched (no launch there).

** The fix

autolisp-front-end/source/backend-cad-common.lisp: a CONSOLE-CAPTURE (two paths
and an external format) made by MAKE-CONSOLE-CAPTURE in the run's workdir --
=engine-console-stdout.txt=, =engine-console-stderr.txt= --
CONSOLE-CAPTURE-LAUNCH-KEYS (:output PATH :if-output-exists :supersede
:error-output PATH :if-error-output-exists :supersede) and
PROCESS-CONSOLE-TEXT (the file's text, then whatever a pipe still holds when a
test launcher returned one). Files rather than reader threads: a file never
fills, reading it can never block -- not even when a grandchild (the
bricscad.exe a cscript started) still holds the handle, which would block a
read of a pipe to its end-of-file -- it needs no thread (same on SBCL/CCL,
Windows/POSIX; it is the shape the clautolisp child already had), and
--keep-workdir leaves the console for a post-mortem. Standard input stays a
pipe.

The diagnostics are unchanged in content: PROCESS-EXIT-DETAILS (AutoCAD
exit-before-READY: exit code + stdout/stderr, NULs of UTF-16LE stripped,
decoded with the same -Econsole / -Ecadstdio / $ALFE_AUTOCAD_CONSOLE_ENCODING
/ ISO-8859-1 choice as the pipe was) and LAUNCHER-FAILURE-DETAILS (BricsCAD,
4000-character tails) now read the capture files.

Documented: alfe user manual, /CAD engine control/; alfe specification,
/Workdir and timeout/ (the engine's console); RELEASE_NOTES (alfe).

** The tests

autolisp-front-end/tests/console-flood-tests.lisp (4 tests, FiveAM =is=):
autocad-batch-console-flood-does-not-block-the-engine,
bricscad-console-flood-does-not-block-the-engine,
autocad-batch-console-flood-keeps-exit-diagnostics,
bricscad-console-flood-keeps-launcher-failure-details. The child is the host
Lisp (SBCL runtime+core, CCL kernel+image), so they run on every lane,
Windows included; a deadlock fails each in 30 s. The Windows-only
autocad-start-engine-batch-surfaces-early-process-exit now passes START-ENGINE's
keywords through instead of forcing :stream pipes.

** To confirm on the runner (why this stays NEXT)

=console-flood:probe:accoreconsole:windows= (.gitlab/native.yml, probe family
=console-flood=; automatic on a merge-request pipeline that changes the probe,
manual otherwise) runs scripts/run-console-flood-probe.ps1:
console-flood-probe.lsp PROMPTs 2, 4, 8, 16, 64 and 256 KB to accoreconsole's
command line in ONE session (--autocad --mode batch --timeout 300,
--keep-workdir). Report dist/console-flood/accoreconsole-Windows.txt: FLOOD-DONE
= complete; FLOOD-INCOMPLETE (the job goes red) = the session stopped, at the
last FLOOD-BEGIN. The kept workdir's engine-console-stdout.txt size shows how
much console text accoreconsole produced. Close this ticket when that job has
reported FLOOD-DONE with this fix; then the optkw probe can go back to one
session (and benchmark:autocad:windows, which also runs --mode batch and died
mid-suite on 2026-09-28, can be re-run against it --
autocad-com-fails-in-disconnected-windows-session.issue).
